tools.description:
Get recursive structure for one or more objects (including layers). Returns 'structures' array. Each entry: uuid, name, object_type, bounds, locked (when true), hidden (when true) — both inherited from ancestors; is_generative (when true), generative_type (when present), has_edit_lineage (when true). Bounds are visual bounds that include live effect expansion (e.g. Gaussian blur expands bounds beyond the path geometry). Bounds may be absent for empty groups. Text objects also include text_content (the full text string). Groups and layers have children (array, z-order: 0=topmost) and child_count. Use maxDepth to limit recursion (groups/layers at max depth show child_count + child_types summary instead of children). Use includeTypes to filter — only matching types appear; groups/layers are always traversed to find matches within the traversed depth. NOTE: includeTypes only matches objects reached within maxDepth — if matching objects exist inside nested groups beyond maxDepth they will not appear. Use maxDepth=-1 to find all matching objects regardless of nesting. Example: maxDepth=2, includeTypes=['text'] finds text objects up to 2 levels deep. CRITICAL: Use this to drill into groups and layers from GetCanvasStructure/GetArtboardStructure. BATCH: Pass array of UUIDs. LARGE RESPONSES: When the response size limit is reached, expansion stops — groups already listed retain child_count/child_types for navigation. Results include truncated=true and resume_hint. Call GetObjectStructure on specific group UUIDs to drill deeper. RESUME: truncation can happen at ANY depth, not just the top level a UUID was requested with — recursively scan every node in 'structures' (including nested 'children', however deep) for partial:true and resume EACH one individually via uuids=[that node's uuid], startAfterUuid=<its resume_after_uuid> (see the startAfterUuid parameter for how multiple uuids/cursors combine in one call). If a node has partial:true but NO resume_after_uuid, either that group's expansion stopped before any child was returned, or (for a text object) its text_content was dropped to fit the size limit (text_length still reflects the true length) — either way, retry with uuids=[that uuid] and no startAfterUuid to get it fresh with its own budget. A uuid in remaining_uuids that is NOT itself marked partial anywhere was never touched at all — re-call with uuids=[that uuid] alone, no cursor needed. RESUME PATTERN — to FULLY expand a list of uuids you MUST drain BOTH mechanisms until nothing is truncated or partial. A single response can carry remaining_uuids AND nested partial:true nodes at the same time; handling only one leaves objects uncollected. Keep two queues: 'cursored' (partial nodes to resume, each with its own resume_after_uuid) and 'fresh' (uuids not yet reached, seeded with the uuids you want to expand). Loop until both are empty. Example Python:
def find_partials(nodes):
    found, stack = [], list(nodes or [])
    while stack:
        n = stack.pop()
        if n.get("partial"):
            found.append((n["uuid"], n.get("resume_after_uuid")))
        stack.extend(n.get("children") or [])
    return found
cursored, fresh, structures = [], list(target_uuids), []
while cursored or fresh:
    kwargs = {"includeTypes": ["text"], "maxDepth": "-1"}
    if cursored:
        uuid, cursor = cursored.pop(0)
        kwargs["uuids"] = [uuid] + fresh
        fresh = []
        if cursor:
            kwargs["startAfterUuid"] = cursor
    else:
        kwargs["uuids"], fresh = fresh, []
    res = GetObjectStructure(**kwargs)
    structures.extend(res.get("structures") or [])
    cursored += find_partials(res.get("structures") or [])
    fresh += res.get("remaining_uuids") or []
Then filter 'structures' (recursively over children) by object_type for your target objects.
